this morning on the dish Timothy  Hollingsworth had humble beginnings in  the food industry born in Texas and  raised in central California his first  restaurant job was as a dishwasher but  he rose through the ranks and to the  highest heights  he became the chef de cuisine at Thomas  Keller's world-renowned French Laundry  and won the James Beard Award for rising  star chef he eventually stepped out on  his own in Los Angeles where his odium  serves vibrant rustic dishes flavored  with the influence of his worldwide  travels Timothy Hollingsworth good  morning welcome to the dish thank you  for having me tell us what's on this  great table yeah we have cauliflower  roasted with almonds capers and grapes  butternut squash with a coffee glaze in  homemade ricotta on far in there you  have a fig and burrata tart with a  little onion jam on the bottom and this  is one of our most famous dishes it's a  herb roasted chicken with shishitos and  soy glaze with tomatoes and Aleppo  seasoned potatoes and then of course for  dessert we have a milfoil  with hazelnuts and vanilla cream of  course all right chef so let's start at  the very beginning humble roots in the  south big family where'd you learn to  love food I think I learned to love food  around the table you know my family that  was a one time that my family was all  together and sitting around the table  and and eating and we have a very close  family still today and I think that's  why I have such a love and care for it  hmm but what I was really struck by in  your story is that when you grew up and  you started out in Texas yeah I moved to  California but but at age 19 I think it  was you flew yourself to New York yeah  there's my first time on an airplane I  flew myself to New York I wanted to go  check out the culinary school at the CIA  in Hyde Park  I ate Atlas circ checked out the  culinary circle Andheri Institute for a  week came back down ada do cost well at  the Attucks what were you telling  yourself at this point honest honest I  don't want to be a chef you know any did  you go from never having been on an  airplane to lucilla Cirque and do cops I  mean that's just that's a big grand  ambitions yes little ignorant too naive  and being young and you know I don't  know amazing why did Thomas Keller  choose you yeah I think he chose me to  get me off his back a little bit  I called and I called him finally he he  said okay come try out and then okay I  winner tried out and then I called I  called I called and then I called it  called and he finally said okay come did  you not let I'm for when I read you  literally went to his with your resume  to the restaurant on a night you knew  he'd be there  I called multiple times to make sure  that he was going to be in the  restaurant that night and I would be  able to turn in my resume to him what  did you learn from him at per se  obviously yeah but I opened a per se but  I learned the the work ethic that he had  the the legacy that he tries to build  the love that he has for the the  industry that we're in is pretty  impeccable and just as detail he's so  detail-oriented every detail matters  yeah you stayed at French Laundry for  thirteen years yes that's a long time in  any restaurant yeah but I think you know  you when you're when you're you know  somewhat comfortable in an environment  you're able to learn more I spent a lot  of years traveling you know I'd work and  then we'd have two weeks off during  those two weeks off I'd go and travel to  France and maybe work for a couple weeks  or England and work for a couple weeks  in other people's restaurants and come  back and apply that knowledge at the  French Laundry and when we talk about  otm your restaurant in LA it says we  know that you've woven in taste from  around the world what are they what if  what have you held on to I mean I think  it's American food I think it's American  food at its finest America's as a you  know melting pot of so many different  cultures that we draw influence from  from who we are Los Angeles there's like  how many different ethnicities and  different cultures and different cities  for different people and you know that's  that's really what American food is all  about American food is about the whole  world it is chef as I asked you to sign  this dish as it's custom here on this  segment I'll ask you the question we  always pose which is if you could share  this meal with anyone past or present  who would it be well that's a tough  question  Elaine Chapelle I think he's one of the  chef's that uh that I read about as a  young cook and having his philosophy  about around food was was so inspiring  and I would have loved it and met him I  would have loved to work for him well  well maybe maybe up in the sky a bounty  chef Timothy Hollingsworth thanks for  your time and congrats on every thank  you and for more on chef Hollingsworth  and the dish head to our website at CBS  this morning dot-com
 

